{:edb/db-before-t 2448 :edb/db-after-t 2449 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535761 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:43:15.160Z" 13194139535761 true] [17592186087705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365095 13194139535761 true] [17592186087705 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365102 13194139535761 true] [17592186087706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365109 13194139535761 true] [17592186087706 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365116 13194139535761 true] [17592186088074 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535761 true] [17592186088074 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨4t+4,6t-21⟩\\,,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨42,\\,-18⟩$ when $t=4,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535761 true] [17592186088074 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$a(t) = ⟨4t + 4, 6t - 21⟩, t > 0$.\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v = ⟨42,-18⟩$ when $t = 4$, calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535761 false] [17592186088074 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨4t+4,6t-21⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (4t+4)\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int (6t-21)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}+4t+{C}_{1},\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨42,\\,-18⟩.$ Substituting $t=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(4) & =⟨2(4)^{2}+4(4)+{C}_{1},\\,3(4)^{2}-21(4)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨42,\\,-18⟩ & =⟨48+{C}_{1},\\,-36+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=-6$ and ${C}_{2}=18.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨2{t}^{2}+4t-6,\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+18⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2}+4t-6,\\,3{t}^{2}-21t+18⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+4t-6=2(t-1)(t+3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=-3,1, \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-21t+18=3(t-1)(t-6) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=1,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=1,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535761 true] [17592186365095 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365096 13194139535761 true] [17592186365095 1089 "q-122086" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365095 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535761 true] [17592186365095 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3{t}^{2},t+\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨1,-1⟩$ when $t=1,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=2.$" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365095 1113 #uuid "36bd380d-dae8-5b76-9cc2-25429c7a8051" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365095 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3{t}^{2},t+\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (t+\\frac{1}{2})\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(1)=⟨1,-1⟩.$ Substituting $t=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨{1}^{3}+{C}_{1},\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,-1⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨{t}^{3},\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨{2}^{3},\\frac{{2}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{2}{2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365097 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365098 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365099 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365100 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365101 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1041 "selection" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365098 13194139535761 true] [17592186365096 1110 #uuid "7e191d0f-5b18-5a36-805a-ed686c90d1be" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365097 1034 "v(2)=⟨4,\\frac{3}{2}⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365097 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365097 1111 #uuid "fcf9bf9f-954c-57b6-a123-694b1a50d9e8" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365098 1034 "v(2)=⟨8,1⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365098 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365098 1111 #uuid "36ff2f93-fb80-5679-aeef-15ab8bfea1b5" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365099 1034 "v(2)=⟨4,-2⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365099 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365099 1111 #uuid "eb9e0fed-379c-5ade-ae18-79c30a3c6439" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365100 1034 "v(2)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365100 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365100 1111 #uuid "bb700b28-d74f-5af7-a1e3-21ad4b4ad474" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365101 1034 "v(2)=⟨8,-2⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365101 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365101 1111 #uuid "5c2af7d6-e366-5356-b2f3-667207aef115" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365102 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365103 13194139535761 true] [17592186365102 1089 "q-122092" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365102 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535761 true] [17592186365102 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,1⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365102 1113 #uuid "d7df19fa-04ae-5351-a8a0-d4ee8b09fbbe" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365102 1145 "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1-3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (4t-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},2{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨2-{2}^{3}+{C}_{1},2({2}^{2})-2+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,1⟩ & =⟨-6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=5$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+5,2{t}^{2}-t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1-{1}^{3}+5,2({1}^{2})-1-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365104 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365105 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365106 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365107 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365108 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1041 "selection" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365108 13194139535761 true] [17592186365103 1110 #uuid "44d4d6ec-9913-5019-aa2b-3fe949dca787" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365104 1034 "v(1)=⟨5,-2⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365104 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365104 1111 #uuid "507c7290-8ad6-5e80-9893-d6cb9be7b884" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365105 1034 "v(1)=⟨3,-2⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365105 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365105 1111 #uuid "10a859fa-7e36-5797-a4bf-c5f6fe12264d" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365106 1034 "v(1)=⟨-1,-4⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365106 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365106 1111 #uuid "c42f42e5-4718-532a-a10e-a289685a31b2" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365107 1034 "v(1)=⟨3,2⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365107 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365107 1111 #uuid "d551da63-3fed-5a9c-9934-1b946500fc60" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365108 1034 "v(1)=⟨5,-4⟩" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365108 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365108 1111 #uuid "3e13ecf5-986b-561f-9f66-8b992b35b37b" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365109 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365110 13194139535761 true] [17592186365109 1089 "q-122097" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365109 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535761 true] [17592186365109 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨\\frac{1}{t-3},2⟩,\\,t>3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2⟩$ when $t=5,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535761 true] [17592186365109 1113 #uuid "be1d00ae-0d99-5592-b106-53e15e2080df" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365109 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\frac{1}{t-3},2⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\frac{1}{t-3}\\,\\text{d}t,\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3)+{C}_{1},2t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(5)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2⟩.$ Substituting $t=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(5) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-3)+{C}_{1},2(5)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,2⟩ & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+{C}_{1},10+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-8.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3),2t-8⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3),2t-8⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t-3) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=4, \\\\ 2t-8=2(t-4) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=4,$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365111 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365112 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365113 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365114 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365115 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1041 "selection" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365113 13194139535761 true] [17592186365110 1110 #uuid "69dfe4a1-c122-520c-b52d-a5393989bc74" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365111 1034 "t=6" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365111 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365111 1111 #uuid "3688b68c-18ba-511c-9e22-058359eb4353" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365112 1034 "t=\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365112 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365112 1111 #uuid "7e29080d-1ea8-58cd-b87d-35c16a0d7273" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365113 1034 "t=4" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365113 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365113 1111 #uuid "38b991eb-5c9e-5da0-b004-b7a9bab76248" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365114 1034 "t=\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365114 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365114 1111 #uuid "9c07faeb-191d-57ab-bf76-696abe41914d" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365115 1034 "No stationary points" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365115 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139535761 true] [17592186365115 1111 #uuid "2b1d9568-db1f-5203-b761-f7036652a8e6" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365116 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365117 13194139535761 true] [17592186365116 1089 "q-83928" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365116 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535761 true] [17592186365116 1100 "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)⟩,\\,0\\le t<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ when $t=0,$ calculate the times at which the particle is stationary." 13194139535761 true] [17592186365116 1113 #uuid "41270051-ca64-57eb-8b58-5bfbd46339fe" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365116 1145 "The particle is stationary when its velocity is zero. So first, we need to compute the velocity of the particle.\n\nTo compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(0)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{C}_{1},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,1+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the times at which the particle is stationary, we solve for the times at which the velocity is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{2}⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) & =0\\,⇒\\,t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\frac{1}{2} & =0\\,⇒\\,t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe only solution that satisfies both equations is $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ so this is the only value of $t$ when the particle is stationary." 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365118 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365119 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365120 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365121 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1000 #edb/ref 17592186365122 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1041 "selection" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1104 #edb/ref 17592186365119 13194139535761 true] [17592186365117 1110 #uuid "09463fad-df2a-5772-9549-1abf1f98c9c2" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365118 1034 "t=0" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365118 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365118 1111 #uuid "510ea6a5-8cda-529e-802a-5c50f1d0600d" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365119 1034 "t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365119 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365119 1111 #uuid "6d57aed9-ad9d-5edd-9b75-5e29f420772a" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365120 1034 "t=\\frac{\\pi }{6}" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365120 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535761 true] [17592186365120 1111 #uuid "96ad4f3d-e14d-5e5d-98f0-a53bdc5f0f31" 13194139535761 true] [17592186365121 1034 "t=0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}" 13194139535761 true] [17